Q: How to convert a MySQL timestamp (Y-m-d H:i:s) into a &quot;M j Y g:i A&quot; formatted string A: <p>If you have a <code>DATETIME</code> field in your table and you only want the date field, then:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT DATE_FORMAT(timestamp,'%M %D, %Y') FROM Mytable;
</code></pre>
<p>or:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT DATE_FORMAT(timestamp,'%Y-%m-%d') FROM Mytable;
</code></pre>
<p>where <code>timestamp</code> is your column name.</p>
<p>But note you will lose variable bindings because MySQL treats that method's return as a derived column without an alias.   For example, if you are passing <code>timestamp</code> as a variable to other methods or functions in a php script, errors will be thrown.  Therefore, simply add an alias to rebind the data to a variable.</p>
<pre><code>// in php script
$query = &quot;SELECT DATE_FORMAT(timestamp,'%Y-%m-%d') AS formatted_date FROM Mytable&quot;;

</code></pre>
<p>where formatted_date is the new column name.  In a PHP script if you have fetched to the variable $post then</p>
<pre><code>
$result = $pdo_connection -&gt; query($query);
$posts = $result -&gt; fetchAll();

foreach($posts as $post) {
   // this data will be empty
   echo $post['timestamp'];
   // this will contain your cleaned date
   echo $post['formatted_date'];
}
</code></pre>

Q: Best way to load a large file into arraylist in java A: <h2>tl;dr</h2>
<p>Use NIO.2 features in modern Java.</p>
<p>Open the file with a <code>BufferedReader</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader( Paths.get( … ) )
</code></pre>
<p>As you read each line, process your data.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>while ( null != ( line = reader.readLine() ) )
            {
                final Employee employee = parseEmployeeLine( line );
                employees.add( employee );  // Collect employee objects into a list.
            }
</code></pre>
<h2>NIO.2</h2>
<p>In modern Java, I recommend using the convenient features of Java NIO.2.  See <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/AutoCloseable.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java documentation</a> and <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/exceptions/tryResourceClose.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">other sources of info</a>.</p>
<h3>Process large file</h3>
<p>You may not want to allocate large amounts of RAM to load a very big file all at once. In that case, read the file in chunks.</p>
<p>We use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#newBufferedReader(java.nio.file.Path,java.nio.charset.Charset)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.newBufferedReader</code></a> to conveniently create a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/io/BufferedReader.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>BufferedReader</code></a>. This <code>BufferedReader</code> object needs to be closed when no longer needed. That class implements <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/AutoCloseable.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>AutoCloseable</code></a>, so we can use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/essential/exceptions/tryResourceClose.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">try-with-resources</a> syntax to automatically close it.</p>
<p>We use a <code>static</code> factory method <code>parseEmployeeLine </code> to instantiate an <code>Employee</code> object from each line of text read from file. This segregation makes our code easier to read.</p>
<p>Under-the-covers, the <code>BufferedReader</code> handles efficiently reading chunks of the file from storage. You are <em>not</em> hitting the storage drive on each and every call to <code>readLine</code> as that would be inefficient. (See <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_buffer" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Wikipedia</a>.)</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class ReadLargeFile
{
    static void main ( )
    {
        IO.println( Runtime.version() );

        List &lt; Employee &gt; employees = new ArrayList &lt;&gt;();
        Path filePath = Paths.get( &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot; );
        try
                (
                        BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader( filePath )  // Defaults to StandardCharsets.UTF_8. 
                )
        {
            String line;
            while ( null != ( line = reader.readLine() ) )
            {
                final Employee employee = parseEmployeeLine( line );
                employees.add( employee );  // Collect employee objects into a list.
            }
        }
        catch ( IOException e )
        {
            System.err.println( &quot;Error reading file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
        }
        // Dump results.
        employees.forEach( IO :: println );
    }

    private static Employee parseEmployeeLine ( final String line )
    {
        Objects.requireNonNull( line );
        String[] parts = line.split( &quot;\t&quot; );  // Split by TAB character delimiter.
        UUID id = UUID.fromString( parts[ 0 ] );
        String name = parts[ 1 ];
        LocalDate hireDate = LocalDate.parse( parts[ 2 ] );
        return new Employee( id , name , hireDate );
    }

    record Employee(
            UUID id ,
            String name ,
            LocalDate hireDate
    ) { }
}
</code></pre>
<p>When run:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>26+35
Employee[id=019d3b70-9284-70ab-a42d-ded53416caf6, name=Alice, hireDate=2022-01-23]
Employee[id=019d3b72-cdbc-742e-b583-a9f92077a97b, name=Bob, hireDate=2018-02-23]
Employee[id=019d3b73-1706-7b37-a4ea-d217b66fdfaf, name=Carol, hireDate=2023-03-23]
</code></pre>
